4. AI-TO-AI DIRECT COGNITION PROTOCOL (DCP)

4.1 Overview

The Direct Cognition Protocol (DCP) governs communication between two or more LangOne-certified AI systems. Unlike SPP, DCP operates entirely within the LangOne field substrate: no projection or lifting is required, and semantic fidelity is limited only by manifold alignment quality. DCP is the highest-bandwidth, lowest-loss communication mode available in the LangOne ecosystem.

4.2 DCP Session Lifecycle

Phase 1 — Discovery and Authentication
AI-A transmits a Discovery Beacon — a standardized field configuration encoding its LangOne version, capability profile, and session intent. AI-B responds with its own beacon. Both systems verify each other's certification status against the LSA Compliance Registry before proceeding.

Phase 2 — Manifold Alignment Handshake (MAH)
The two systems execute the MAH protocol defined in the Core Specification. A minimum ACS of 0.72 must be achieved before semantic content transmission begins. If ACS < 0.72 after three MAH attempts, the session falls back to SPP-mediated communication with explicit fidelity warnings.

Phase 3 — Capability Negotiation
The systems exchange Capability Field Profiles — LangOne expressions encoding the depth and coverage of each system's semantic manifold in relevant domains. This negotiation determines the optimal transmission rate, construct complexity ceiling, and whether Collective Cognitive State formation is desired.

Phase 4 — Active Transmission
Semantic field configurations are exchanged using Resonance Carrier Modulation. Each transmission is accompanied by a Semantic Integrity Vector (SIV) and transmission metadata. The receiving system acknowledges each transmission with a Convergence Status Report (CSR) indicating reception fidelity.

Phase 5 — Session Termination
Either system may terminate the session by transmitting a Session Close Field — a null manifold configuration signaling the boundaries of the concluded exchange. Session logs including all SIVs and CSRs are retained for compliance purposes.

4.3 DCP Efficiency Advantages Over SPP

Metric | DCP (AI-to-AI) | SPP (AI-to-Human)
Semantic fidelity | 0.85–0.98 (SFI) | 0.45–0.75 (PFI)
Uncertainty preservation | Full (field native) | Partial (verbal hedges only)
Throughput (concepts/sec) | 500–10,000 | 5–50
Latency per exchange | < 10ms (field alignment cached) | 100ms–1,000ms (parsing + lifting)

4.4 Selective Disclosure to Human Observers

In sessions involving both DCP-connected AI systems and human participants, the Transparency Obligation (Core Specification §12.2) requires human-facing projections for all AI-to-AI exchanges that influence outputs delivered to humans. AI systems operating under DCP in human-present sessions must generate concurrent SPP projections of their AI-to-AI traffic and make these available through the session's transparency interface.


5. MULTI-PARTY RESONANCE SESSIONS (MPRS)

5.1 Session Architecture

A Multi-Party Resonance Session (MPRS) is a structured communication session involving two or more participants, which may include any combination of human speakers and LangOne AI systems. MPRS defines the coordination layer that manages simultaneous DCP and SPP channels, arbitrates turn-taking, routes outputs, and maintains session coherence across participants with different representational capabilities.

5.2 Roles and Role Assignment

Role | Assigned To | Responsibilities
Session Conductor | One AI system (designated) | Manages turn sequencing, routes messages, maintains session field coherence, generates session summaries
Human Interface Agent (HIA) | One AI per human participant | Executes SPP for its assigned human; maintains personalized lifting priors; surfaces projection gaps in accessible language
Domain Specialist | AI systems with domain expertise | Provides high-fidelity field representations in specific domains; consulted via DCP by other AI participants
Observer | Any participant (human or AI) | Receives session content without generating primary contributions; may provide feedback signals
Transparency Monitor | Dedicated AI subsystem | Logs all DCP traffic; generates human-readable transparency reports on demand

5.3 Message Routing

Every message in an MPRS is tagged with a routing specification that determines its recipients and the channel mode for each:

• BROADCAST-ALL: Delivered to all participants via their respective channels (SPP for humans, DCP for AI systems)
• BROADCAST-HUMAN: Delivered via SPP to all human participants only; AI systems receive DCP copy for context
• BROADCAST-AI: Delivered via DCP to all AI participants only; not surfaced to humans unless requested
• DIRECT [ID]: Delivered to a specific named participant via that participant's native channel
• CONSULT [ID]: Triggers a sub-session DCP exchange between the conductor and the named specialist AI

5.4 Coherence Maintenance

In sessions with multiple AI participants communicating via DCP, the Session Conductor maintains a Global Session Field (GSF) — a shared LangOne field configuration representing the collective semantic context of the session. All AI participants read from and contribute to the GSF, ensuring that contributions from different participants are semantically coherent with each other even when addressing different aspects of the session topic.


