10. EFFICIENCY METRICS AND OPTIMIZATION

10.1 Session Efficiency Index (SEI)

The Session Efficiency Index (SEI) is a composite metric for evaluating MPRS performance. It is computed as:

SEI = (SFI_avg × PFI_avg × throughput) / (latency × PGR_avg)

Benchmark: higher values indicate more efficient sessions; production deployments target SEI ≥ 0.65.

10.2 Optimization Strategies

GSF Pre-loading
For predictable session types such as recurring crisis coordination or standing policy committees, pre-initialize the GSF with domain-specific field configurations before human participants join. This reduces time-to-first-useful-output by 40–60%.

LAM Domain Extension
Maintain participant-specific Lexical Anchor Maps that extend the baseline LAM with domain-specific vocabulary. Pre-compute LAM extensions for known participant profiles. This reduces lifting latency by up to 35%.

Parallel SPP Generation
In multi-human sessions, generate all participant-specific SPP projections in parallel after each DCP synthesis cycle rather than sequentially. This is essential for maintaining sub-60-second SLA in crisis sessions.

Cached MAH Alignment
Persist MAH alignment matrices between sessions with the same AI participants. Re-alignment is needed only if a participant system has been updated since the last session. This eliminates 200–800ms per-session MAH overhead for established AI teams.

Selective Transparency Logging
Log full DCP traffic but generate human-readable transparency reports only on demand for non-crisis sessions. This reduces transparency-monitor computational overhead by up to 70% without violating the Transparency Obligation.


11. ERROR HANDLING AND RECOVERY

11.1 Error Classification

Error Type | Trigger Condition | Recovery Protocol
MAH Failure | ACS < 0.50 after 3 attempts | Fall back to SPP-mediated AI-to-AI; log misalignment signature for offline analysis
Semantic Divergence (SDA) | SFI drops below 0.40 mid-session | Pause transmission; re-execute partial MAH for affected domain; resume with reduced complexity ceiling
Lifting Failure | LCS < 0.30 | Reject lifting; transmit clarification request to human via HIA; do not generate a field from insufficient input
Projection Gap Overflow | PGR > 0.40 | Decompose the expression into sub-fields; project the largest coherent subset; explicitly notify human that significant content could not be projected
GSF Incoherence | Cross-AI field consistency score < 0.55 | Conductor calls for GSF synchronization cycle; all AI participants re-broadcast current field states; conductor re-synthesizes before the next response
Participant Dropout | AI system disconnects mid-session | Conductor reconstructs missing AI's last field contribution from GSF history; marks affected outputs with reduced-fidelity flag; continues session

11.2 Human-Visible Error Communication

Errors in LangOne processing must never be silently suppressed or compensated for without notification. When a recoverable error occurs and the session continues, human participants must receive a plain-language notification through their HIA within one response cycle. The notification must: (1) state that a communication quality issue occurred; (2) describe in accessible terms what semantic content may be affected; and (3) indicate whether the system has compensated and how.

Example HIA error notification:
"I should note that one part of my analysis in the previous response drew on a domain (industrial process chemistry) where my knowledge model and the specialist AI's model did not align well. The cost figures in that section are less reliable than the rest of my response. I recommend treating those numbers as indicative only and consulting a specialized source for final figures."


